## THE FAIL — attempt-3's own explicit contract line is not met: pre-existing
## kinds' rendered HTML is NOT byte-identical to master

Ruling KD-2026-08-30d (which IS attempt 3's governing, specific scope)
enumerates, as a required deliverable alongside the two signed-row tests:
"template {{- -}} trim markers so pre-existing kinds' rendered HTML is
byte-identical to master (K5, attempt-2 regression was inert whitespace)."
The manifest claims this was done and independently verified: "Traced by
hand: for the false path (the four pre-existing kinds...), the trimmed
output is now byte-identical to what a single unconditional line would
have produced — matching the pre-KD1 baseline exactly."

This claim is false. PROOF: captured `/dashboard/kpi/expenses` rendered
HTML (via the real renderer, `setupTestEnvWithRenderer`, a fixed 3-month
fixture) from master (`git stash` the four KD1 files in the scratch copy,
capture, `git stash pop`) and from attempt 3, then diffed byte for byte:
master = 101 lines, attempt 3 = 102 lines — exactly one extra blank line,
inserted immediately before the first `<tr>` in `<tbody>`. Root cause:
attempt 2/3 added a new unconditional template line,
`{{$noCoverage := and (eq .Type "healthcare") .HealthcareNoCoverageInRange}}`
(kpi-detail.html, right after the pre-existing `{{$avg}}`/`{{$type}}`/
`{{$isRate}}` assignment lines), with no `{{- -}}` trim markers — unlike the
two `{{if}}/{{else}}/{{end}}` blocks the manifest describes trimming, this
assignment line was never touched, and it executes for every kpiType
(income/expenses/savings/savings-rate too, not just the two new kinds),
so it is not confined to "healthcare" despite the variable's name. The
worker's "traced by hand" claim addressed only the two if/else/end blocks
it explicitly lists, not this third new line — the actual full-render
comparison was evidently never run.

No test in the suite catches this: `TestHandleKPIDetail_ExpensesResponseByteIdentical`
asserts the JSON response (via `setupTestEnv`, no renderer) which is
unaffected by template whitespace, so K5's own literal text ("responses
byte-identical") is satisfied even though the ruling's rendered-HTML
commitment is not — the gap escapes the shipped suite entirely, matching
this constitution's warning that worker manifests overclaim verification
the suite doesn't actually check.

Impact assessment (for proportionality, not to pre-empt the lead's call):
a whitespace-only text node between block-level elements has zero visual
rendering difference in any browser, zero DOM-structural difference for
any script querying by tag/class, and zero accessibility-tree difference —
this is the same "inert" characterization the ruling itself uses for the
attempt-2 instance of this defect class. The fix is a one-line change
(add `{{- -}}` to the new assignment, or fold it inside the `{{if}}` that
already needs it). I am not asserting user-facing harm; I am asserting the
attempt's own explicit, written acceptance line is unmet and the
manifest's specific verification claim about it is false.

## Scope
In scope: kpi-detail.html is one of KD1's listed files; the offending line
was added by KD1 (attempt 2, retained in attempt 3); the "byte-identical to
master" commitment was written into THIS attempt's own governing ruling
(KD-2026-08-30d), not an earlier general ruling being second-guessed.
Fixable without exceeding KD1's file list.

## Recommended remedy (not prescriptive)
Trim the new `{{$noCoverage := ...}}` line, e.g.
`{{- $noCoverage := and (eq .Type "healthcare") .HealthcareNoCoverageInRange -}}`,
or move it inside the `{{else}}` branch of the header's type switch where
it's actually needed. Re-diff master vs. all four pre-existing kinds'
rendered HTML (not just JSON) before re-claiming byte-identity.
